                             contributes 0.\\n    -   So, sum                   
                             `count_lt(n, m)` for all $m$ such that             
                             `reducible[m][k]` is True.\\n\\n###                
                             Computing `count_lt(n, m)`:\\n-                    
                             Iterate through the bits of $n$ from               
                             left to right.\\n-   Maintain `cnt` =              
                             number of 1s placed so far in the                  
                             prefix.\\n-   When we encounter a \'1\'            
                             in $n$ at position $i$ (0-indexed from             
                             left), we can choose to put \'0\' at               
                             this position and any combination of               
                             bits for the remaining positions such              
                             that total set bits is $m$.\\n    -                
                             If we put \'0\', then we need to choose            
                             $m - cnt$ ones from the remaining $L -             
                             1 - i$ positions.\\n    -   Number of              
                             ways = $\\\\binom{L - 1 - i}{m -                   
                             cnt}$.\\n    -   If $m - cnt < 0$ or $m            
                             - cnt > L - 1 - i$, ways = 0.\\n    -              
                             After considering \'0\', we proceed                
                             with \'1\' (following the tight                    
                             constraint) and increment `cnt`.\\n-               
                             After the loop, if we followed the                 
                             tight constraint all the way, the                  
                             number $n$ itself is not included                  
                             (since we want $< n$). If the final                
                             `cnt` equals $m$, then $n$ has popcount            
                             $m$, but we don\'t count it.\\n-                   
                             Precompute binomial coefficients                   
                             $\\\\binom{n}{k}$ modulo $10^9 + 7$ for            
                             $n$ up to 800.\\n\\n### Steps:\\n1.                
                             Precompute `reducible[v][k]` for $v                
                             \\\\in [1, 800]$ and $k \\\\in [0,                 
